# REGRESSION — `on_error_blocks` is FUNCTION-scoped and never popped, so a
# handler registered inside an `if`/`while` is live to function exit.
# Seeding only the enclosing block left every later statement walked
# blind: rc 0 GARBAGE on both lanes.
#
# The liveness walker is UNDER-APPROXIMATION-CRITICAL: a use it cannot
# see makes a variable look dead at an earlier consuming position, so
# the value is MOVED instead of cloned and the later read finds freed
# memory. RED-verified against the pre-fix compiler.
#
# The `v.set(0, ...)` is load-bearing: it frees the value moved into
# the vector. Without it the moved-from read still finds live memory.
# PROBE N1 -- the `on error:` handler is registered inside an `if` BODY,
# and the
# name it reads is consumed by a statement in the ENCLOSING block, after the
# `if`. `on_error_blocks` is function-scoped and never popped
# (src/ir/lowering/context.rs:251, stmts/mod.rs:460), so the handler stays
# armed
# for every error exit lowered after it -- including the `throw` below.
#
# The `v.set(0, ...)` is load-bearing: it frees the value moved into the
# vector.
String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

int f(int n) throws String:
    String s = mk("abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789", "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ")
    if n > 0:
        on error:
            print(s)
    Vector[String] v = []
    v.push(s)
    v.set(0, mk("xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx", "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy"))
    if n > 0:
        throw "boom"
    return 1

void main():
    int r = f(1)! catch (e): -1
    print(r)
